---
import Layout from '~/layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from 'astrowind:config';

const crumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', href: '/' },
  { name: 'R&D Center', href: '/randdcenter' },
  { name: 'Prototype Workshop' },
];

const metadata = {
  title: `Prototype Workshop — Full-Scale SUP Prototyping & Sea Trials | ${SITE.name}`,
  description: `From CAD to water-ready board in 8-12 weeks. Our Prototype Workshop builds, rigs, and sea-trials custom inflatable SUPs. Start your prototype project today.`,
};
---

<Layout metadata={metadata} breadcrumbs={crumbs}>
  <section class="px-6 sm:px-6 py-16 sm:py-20 mx-auto max-w-5xl">
    <a href="/randdcenter" class="text-sm text-gray-500 hover:underline">&larr; Back to R&D Center</a>
    <div class="mt-4 grid md:grid-cols-5 gap-10">
      <div class="md:col-span-3">
        <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-primary uppercase tracking-wider">Lab 05</p>
        <h1 class="mt-2 text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold">Prototype Workshop</h1>
        <p class="mt-3 text-lg text-muted">From CAD model to water-ready board — built, fitted, and proven</p>
      </div>
      <div class="md:col-span-2">
        <img
          src="https://assets.afarer.com/images/afarer/products/sup-series/sup-ocean-voyager-1-1200w.webp"
          alt="afarer prototype workshop board fabrication"
          class="w-full h-48 object-cover rounded-xl"
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="px-6 sm:px-6 pb-12 mx-auto max-w-4xl prose prose-gray dark:prose-invert">
    <h2>Where design becomes reality</h2>
    <p>
      A CAD model, no matter how detailed, cannot reveal how a board will actually handle until it meets the water. That
      is why our Prototype Workshop is designed to translate engineering data into a physical, water-ready board as quickly
      and accurately as possible. Every new {SITE.name} model — and every significant custom build — passes through this workshop
      before the production tooling is committed.
    </p>
    <p>
      The workshop is fully equipped for simultaneous fabrication of hull, rails, and rigging. This parallel-workflow
      approach cuts prototype lead time to 8-12 weeks from engineering release, compared to the industry norm of 16-20
      weeks. When your application demands a specialised solution, we can move from concept to on-water validation in
      under three months.
    </p>

    <h2>Workshop capabilities</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Workstation</th><th>Equipment</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr
         ><td>Hull fabrication</td><td
            >CNC fabric cutter, heat-lamination press, drop-stitch core preparation station</td
           ><td>Full-scale hull production in all three construction types</td></tr
         >
        <tr
         ><td>Rail &amp; chamber assembly</td><td>RF welding press, hot-air tacking station, inflation test jig</td><td
            >Fabrication of full-size rail and chamber assemblies with all baffles and valves</td
           ></tr
         >
        <tr
         ><td>Rigging &amp; fitting</td><td
            >Fin-box routing jig, cargo-mount and handle welding fixtures, deck-pad cutting station</td
           ><td>Complete outfitting: fins, cargo mounts, leash plugs, handles, deck pads, valves</td></tr
         >
        <tr
         ><td>Instrumentation bay</td><td
            >GPS speed sensor, pressure transducers, accelerometers, thermocouple array, data-logger</td
           ><td>Sea-trial instrumentation installation and calibration</td></tr
         >
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>The prototype journey — step by step</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Engineering handover</strong> — The design package (3D model, FEA report, bill of materials, layup schedule)
        is formally reviewed by the workshop lead and the project engineer. Any manufacturing concerns are flagged and resolved
        before cutting begins.
      </li>
       <li>
         <strong>Hull production</strong> — The hull is built according to the engineering specification. For single-layer
         construction: the drop-stitch core is laminated with the specified PVC skin. For double-layer construction: the
         additional skin is applied over the base laminate before the pattern is cut. For fused construction: the
         lamination parameters are loaded from the approved process sheet and verified on a first-off coupon.
       </li>
       <li>
         <strong>Rail fabrication</strong> — Fabric is cut using CNC-pattern templates, then RF-welded into full-size rails and chambers.
         Each chamber is inflated to test pressure and left for 24 h for initial stabilisation.
       </li>
       <li>
         <strong>Assembly &amp; rigging</strong> — Hull and rails are mated. Fin boxes and cargo mounts are fitted, leash
         plugs and carry handles are installed, the inflation valve is torqued in, and the deck pad is applied and aligned.
       </li>
       <li>
         <strong>Static tests</strong> — Before the board touches water, it undergoes: buoyancy check (full load + 30 % reserve),
         chamber pressure-hold test, valve leak-down test, handle and mount pull-test.
       </li>
      <li><strong>Sea trial programme</strong> –20 hours of in-water testing, detailed below.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Prototype review</strong> — A formal sign-off meeting is held with engineering, production, and quality teams.
        Any required design changes are documented and incorporated into the production release.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <div class="not-prose my-10 p-6 rounded-xl bg-gray-50 dark:bg-gray-900 border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-800">
      <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-500 mb-3 uppercase tracking-wider">
        20-Hour Sea Trial Programme — Test Schedule
      </p>
      <div class="space-y-2 text-sm">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 p-2 rounded bg-white dark:bg-gray-950">
           <span class="w-12 text-xs font-bold text-blue-600">2 h</span><span class="flex-1"
             >Flat-water familiarisation — low-speed tracking, turning, stance and trim check</span
           >
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 p-2 rounded bg-white dark:bg-gray-950">
           <span class="w-12 text-xs font-bold text-blue-600">4 h</span><span class="flex-1"
             >Speed trial — GPS-verified top speed, cruise speed, sustained pace at 3 stroke rates</span
           >
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 p-2 rounded bg-white dark:bg-gray-950">
           <span class="w-12 text-xs font-bold text-blue-600">3 h</span><span class="flex-1"
             >Turning test — pivot-turn and sweep-turn radius, board response, port and starboard</span
           >
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 p-2 rounded bg-white dark:bg-gray-950">
           <span class="w-12 text-xs font-bold text-blue-600">3 h</span><span class="flex-1"
             >Chop test — repeated runs through 0.5–1.0 m chop; accelerometer at the rider stance position</span
           >
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 p-2 rounded bg-white dark:bg-gray-950">
           <span class="w-12 text-xs font-bold text-blue-600">3 h</span><span class="flex-1"
             >Stability test — static heel angle with 4 riders of varying weight, offset load to starboard</span
           >
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 p-2 rounded bg-white dark:bg-gray-950">
           <span class="w-12 text-xs font-bold text-blue-600">3 h</span><span class="flex-1"
             >Endurance run — 3 h continuous at cruise pace; monitoring of chamber pressure, rail temperature, and rider fatigue</span
           >
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 p-2 rounded bg-white dark:bg-gray-950">
           <span class="w-12 text-xs font-bold text-blue-600">2 h</span><span class="flex-1"
             >Final inspection — post-trial inspection of all components, tighten/check all fasteners, document findings</span
           >
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2>Custom prototyping for unique applications</h2>
    <p>
       The Prototype Workshop is where we solve the problems that standard production boards cannot address. Recent
       examples include:
     </p>
     <ul>
       <li>
         A high-visibility rescue SUP for a search-and-rescue organisation, requiring integrated grab handles, gear
         mounts for rescue equipment, and a high-buoyancy hull for casualty recovery — all prototyped, fitted, and
         tested at our facility.
       </li>
       <li>
         An expedition touring SUP for a hydrographic survey contractor, with a removable instrument mast, sealed gear
         ports, and a low-profile transducer mount designed to minimise drag.
       </li>
       <li>
         A compact fishing SUP for a resort fleet, where the prototype was used to validate stability, gear rigging,
         and durability under daily rental use before the client committed to a fleet of four.
       </li>
     </ul>

    <h2>Our prototyping team</h2>
    <p>
       The workshop is staffed by eight multi-skilled marine technicians, each cross-trained in at least two of the three
       core disciplines (hull, rail, rigging). The team lead is a master fabricator with 16 years of experience spanning
      custom one-off builds to production series. Every prototype is assigned a dedicated project engineer who serves as
      the single point of contact for the client throughout the build and trial process.
    </p>

    <h2>Client involvement</h2>
    <p>
      We actively encourage our clients to attend key milestones during the prototype build — particularly the initial
      fit-out review and the sea trial. Your operational feedback during these stages is invaluable in fine-tuning the
       board before it enters production. We do not build in a vacuum; we build in partnership with you.
     </p>
     <p>
       The 20-hour sea trial is also an opportunity for your team to familiarise themselves with the board's handling,
       deck layout, and maintenance requirements, ensuring a smooth transition to service when the production boards
       are delivered.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="px-6 sm:px-6 pb-20 mx-auto max-w-4xl text-center">
    <div class="rounded-xl border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-800 bg-gray-50 dark:bg-gray-900 p-8">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold">Start your prototype project</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-muted">
         Tell us about the board you need and we will propose a prototype development plan.
      </p>
      <a
        href="/contact"
        class="mt-4 inline-block px-6 py-3 bg-primary text-white font-semibold rounded-md hover:bg-primary/90"
        >Contact our prototyping team</a
      >
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
